       The example program demonstrates the use of mmaatthheerrrr() when calling
       lloogg(3).  The program takes up to three command‐line arguments.  The
       first argument is the floating‐point number to be given to lloogg(3).  If
       the optional second argument is provided, then __LLIIBB__VVEERRSSIIOONN is set to
       __SSVVIIDD__ so that mmaatthheerrrr() is called, and the integer supplied in the
       command‐line argument is used as the return value from mmaatthheerrrr().  If
       the optional third command‐line argument is supplied, then it specifies
       an alternative return value that mmaatthheerrrr() should assign as the return
       value of the math function.

       The following example run, where lloogg(3) is given an argument of 0.0,
       does not use mmaatthheerrrr():

           $ ..//aa..oouutt 00..00
           errno: Numerical result out of range
           x=-inf

       In the following run, mmaatthheerrrr() is called, and returns 0:

           $ ..//aa..oouutt 00..00 00
           matherr SING exception in log() function
                   args:   0.000000, 0.000000
                   retval: -340282346638528859811704183484516925440.000000
           log: SING error
           errno: Numerical argument out of domain
           x=-340282346638528859811704183484516925440.000000

       The message "log: SING error" was printed by the C library.

       In the following run, mmaatthheerrrr() is called, and returns a nonzero value:

           $ ..//aa..oouutt 00..00 11
           matherr SING exception in log() function
                   args:   0.000000, 0.000000
                   retval: -340282346638528859811704183484516925440.000000
           x=-340282346638528859811704183484516925440.000000

       In this case, the C library did not print a message, and _e_r_r_n_o was not
       set.

       In the following run, mmaatthheerrrr() is called, changes the return value of
       the math function, and returns a nonzero value:

           $ ..//aa..oouutt 00..00 11 1122334455..00
